2	// The people on the active sheet, in the order they were added, and the one form that both adds
3	// a person and edits an existing one (opening a person's editor fills the form with their values).
4	// A person's grade is dated: their first applies from the beginning of time, and each save records
5	// the form's grade from its from-date (the sheet's today when blank), keeping those recorded before;
6	// a save leaving the grade as the row shows it, with the from-date blank, records none. The row
7	// shows the grade in force on the sheet's today; each claim uses the one in force on its date.
8	// A person may have their own terms, a whole number of days (blank for none), shown on the row; a
9	// claim's due-by is fixed by the server when it is created, from these before the grade's or sheet's.
10	// A person may have a contact (blank for none), stored trimmed and lower-cased and shown on the row;
11	// a contact another person on the sheet already has is refused.
12	// The editor remembers the version of the person it opened; if someone else has saved the person
13	// since, the save is refused and the form is refilled with the values the store holds now.
14	// Each row shows what the person has claimed and lists their claims; the sheet's claimed-total
15	// adds everyone's up. Only OPEN, PENDING and SETTLED claims count toward any of these totals. Each claim shows
16	// what is due at the rate for the person's grade in force on the claim's date (a settled claim the
17	// figure fixed when settled), each person the sum of those figures over the claims that count, and
18	// the sheet's due-total adds up every person's due-total that has one. A live due is never below
19	// the floor in force on the claim's date (a figure fixed when settled, bound by a statement or by closing a period keeps its own).
20	// Taking a person's statement binds each of their claims that counts to the figure it has then
21	// (a stated claim shows and totals that figure); each row shows how many statements the person
22	// has taken, and the last one's due-total and date. A steward can cancel the last statement: the
23	// claims it bound are live again, and the row shows the statement before it (or none). A statement
24	// can be taken as of a date on or before the sheet's today: it binds the claims that counted at the
25	// end of that date to their figures as they stood then, and shows that date as its date.
26	// Each claim shows the period its due-by falls in, and each period the due of the claims that counted in it
27	// at its end (by the status each had then, as its log records it).
28	// A person can be retired from a date (the sheet's today when left blank); with a date set they count
29	// as retired at once, whatever the date. No claim of theirs can be dated after it. Retired people are
30	// left out of the list unless "show retired" is on. Once the sheet's today reaches their retirement
31	// date their OPEN and PENDING claims leave every live total, as a hold's would (unless a statement binds them).
32	// A person can be put on hold from a date (the sheet's today when left blank) to an optional to-date,
33	// and may have several holds over time; a hold ends on its to-date. Their OPEN and PENDING claims dated
34	// within a hold count in no total (their own, the sheet's or a period's); their other claims still do,
35	// and so does a claim bound by a statement, whatever its date. A period counts a claim open at its
36	// end but since settled or voided whatever its holds. "On hold" shows whether they have any hold.
37	// The quick-add bar adds a DRAFT claim of an amount, dated the sheet's today with an empty note, for
38	// the person of a name, adding the person with grade A first when the name is not on the sheet.
39	// The paste box takes lines of name,amount,date,note (the date blank for the sheet's today, the note
40	// optional and free to hold commas) and adds a DRAFT claim per line, in order, for the person of that
